{"kind":"report","text":"Decision on 1519db76 (planner, 2026-10-21): v2 layout under both the node and the metadata caps. This decision replaces items 2, 3, 4, 6 and 7 of 6bcb546e. Item 1 (the finding) and item 5 (no earlier v2 archive) still apply.\n\n1. The finding stands. 6bcb546e checked the metadata cap and missed the 64-node cap. By the charge sites in 1519db76, a pass spends 2U + 9 nodes, where U is the number of trees that it changes. One digit for each level gives U = 41 and 91 nodes. Six radix-64 levels in three namespaces give 61 nodes before native inventory. That leaves too little room for inventory and for more than one retained kind. So the radix-64 proposal is not adopted.\n\n2. The cause is three namespaces. Each pass changes one numeric path in each of `passes/`, `segments/` and `retained/`. The adopted layout gives each pass one directory, and keeps all of its files there. Then a pass changes one numeric path.\n\n3. Adopted grammar. `\u003cfirst\u003e` stays 12 decimal digits, and the highest position stays 999,999,999,999. `\u003cp1\u003e` to `\u003cp5\u003e` are its first five pairs of digits.\n   - `journal/v2/head.json`, as now.\n   - `journal/v2/passes/\u003cp1\u003e/\u003cp2\u003e/\u003cp3\u003e/\u003cp4\u003e/\u003cp5\u003e/\u003cfirst\u003e/pass.json`: the pass manifest.\n   - `journal/v2/passes/\u003cp1\u003e/.../\u003cp5\u003e/\u003cfirst\u003e/\u003csegment first\u003e.jsonl`: each segment, named by the 12 digits of its own first position.\n   - `journal/v2/passes/\u003cp1\u003e/.../\u003cp5\u003e/\u003cfirst\u003e/\u003ckind\u003e/\u003cdigest\u003e`: each retained input that the pass wrote. A value has its `d_` domain directory before the digest, as in v1 (journal-path.ts:27-35, :51).\n   Each pair directory has a two-digit name, 00 to 99. So each numeric tree lists at most 100 names. The kinds are definition, entry, rule, snapshot and value. None of them can equal `pass.json` or a segment name.\n\n4. Node bound. A pass changes the root, `journal` and `v2`; `passes` and five pair directories; its own `\u003cfirst\u003e` directory; one directory for each kind that it writes (k); and one for each value domain that it writes (m). So U = 10 + k + m, and the pass spends 29 + 2(k + m) nodes before native inventory. A pass with no retained input spends 29. Witness 7b's pass, with one retained input of one kind, spends 31. This bound is arithmetic from the charge sites in 1519db76. It is not a measurement.\n\n5. Metadata bound. By the fee in 85f523f2, a numeric tree of at most 100 names costs at most 231,552. A pass rewrites six numeric trees, so at most 1,389,312, about a third of the metadata budget, at any position. The trees of the pass's own directory are new, and the pass's cutoff charges them as it now charges any write.\n\n6. Unchanged. Every cap, debit, deadline and the append validation at send stay as they are. There is no cache or validation credit. A retained file stays in the directory of the pass that wrote it, and no later pass moves it. The `pass` column and rules 6 and 9 of 2.3 do not change. Witness 2's key from the pass at 3,700 keeps `pass` 3,700. Its file is `journal/v2/passes/00/00/00/00/37/000000003700/\u003ckind\u003e/\u003cdigest\u003e`.\n\n7. Reader. Rule 7 of 2.4 now refuses each of these:\n   - a numeric directory whose name is not two digits;\n   - a `\u003cfirst\u003e` directory whose pairs differ from its parent directories;\n   - a name in a pass directory that is not `pass.json`, a segment of that pass, or a kind directory;\n   - a segment whose first position is outside the pass's entries.\n   Rules 3 and 4 keep their meaning: the pass that wrote a retained file holds it in its own directory. The reader charges each tree that it reads to its work bound (rule 8). The other reader rules do not change.\n\n8. Conversion. The conversion in 2.5 writes the v1 manifest, segments and retained files into the directory of the pass from 0: `journal/v2/passes/00/00/00/00/00/000000000000/`. Its charges are those of a pass, including one node pair for each kind and value domain.\n\n9. Witnesses for F1.\n   a. In witness 2, the probe asserts that each numeric tree that a pass rewrites lists at most 100 names. It also records the nodes that each pass spends. The pass from 10,000 makes a new `\u003cp4\u003e` directory, and the probe covers it.\n   b. The Git-layer test from 6bcb546e stays, with this grammar: each numeric directory on the path for position 999,999,999,999 is full, with the directories of positions 999,999,999,900 to 999,999,999,998 in place. A pass of one entry with one new retained input fits the original budget. The test reports nodes and metadata. It is Git-layer evidence only, with test-authored trees.\n   c. New: one pass of the native Grow fixture writes every retained kind and value domain that the fixture's entries name. The F1 report states that pass's nodes, the native inventory's node charge, and the largest k + m that one entry can name and still fit. If one Grow entry alone does not fit, report it to the planner.\n   d. Witness 3 refuses each case in item 7.\n\n10. The note. This decision amends sections 2.2, 2.3 rules 4 and 7, 2.4 rules 3, 4 and 7, 2.5 items 2 to 4, and witnesses 2 and 3. A later planner head corrects the note's text, together with 18ef695b.\n\nNo deployment.","body":{"to":"builder"}}